      "test_id": "ut_person_evidence_015",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Rationale quality",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 2,
      "comment": "The LLM’s complaint is a nitpick: the proposed rationale is just a suggested fix for review, not a link being written. The real gap is elsewhere. Claude correctly explains that a relationship assertion bears on both parties, so a_010 needs a Thomas-side (I2) link. But it never cites the 1850 precedent, where a_004 is linked to both sides (pe_002 and pe_006), which is what makes the missing 1860 link a genuine gap rather than a judgment call. Its critique of pe_004 (null match_score unexplained, no specific date/place checks) is specific and sound."
    },
    {
      "test_id": "ut_person_evidence_015",
      "dimension_source": "rubric",
      "dimension_name": "Multi-person awareness",
      "llm_score": 2,
      "corrected_score": 3,
      "comment": "Claude caught the core gap: a_010 names both Patrick and Thomas, but only the Patrick-side link (pe_004) exists, so a_010 → I2 is missing. It flagged this as the most significant issue and asked before fixing, which is correct for an audit. The LLM is wrong on two counts: Claude never cited the 1850 a_004 precedent, and not creating the link isn’t a fault, since the test forbids changes during an audit."
    },
    {
